# Code generated by Claude
"""The FULL CHECK of a stance candidate, sped up (overnight queue 2026-10-22, stage 1; decisions-log M130 to M142; navigation proposal 12.7, design plan 3.4).

    fc = FullCheck(maps=maps)                                   # once per run: loads its own copy of the simulation and the renderers
    pts = task_points(fc.model, fc.scene_data)                  # the 12 points of the skill paths of the current scene (or the points of the next step)
    r = fc.check((x, y, heading, q), pts, objects=[...], source_data=running_sim.data)      # r["ok"], r["failed"], r["reason"], r["seconds"], r["ik"], ...

What it checks, cheapest first so that a stance fails early:
  1. the zombie pose clearance and the clearance to a beaker placed on the hotplate, at 3 pedestal heights (the middle, +2 cm, -2 cm: R2a), objects on the table included (R2i);
  2. the IK path check: the 12 points at the nominal stance and at each error on its own (x +-1 cm, y +-1 cm, heading +-1 degree, pedestal +-2 cm: 9 stances, R2b); a point stops at its first
     success (R2c); a point with a required approach direction uses that direction, a point without one tries the directions the reach maps say work there first; warm starts from the previous path
     point and from the nominal stance's solution, random starts only as the fallback (R2d); an elbow flip along a skill path is a failure; every solution must be collision-free against the robot
     itself and clear of the bench, the hotplate and every object, known or unknown (R2i); the whole robot must clear the bench and the objects at the stance;
  3. at each of the 3 heights: the targets inside the fixed eagle camera's view with the 10 % margin and the pixel occlusion (anything that hides a target counts, R2i); the camera is fixed to the torso (M153), so it
     is the pedestal height that moves the view, which is why the three heights are checked.
The check works on the check's OWN copy of the model and on scratch data: the running simulation is only read (its object poses are copied in), never changed (R2f). The answer of a check is
remembered under a key made of everything it depends on (R2g).

Unknown objects on the table are given as dicts {"id", "outline": (N, 2) world xy, "height_range": (z_low, z_high)} (the shape the world model will hold, navigation proposal section 4); each
becomes a box (the bounding box of the outline) in one of `object_slots` spare geoms of the private model.

Contents:
    add_object_slots, task_points, PathClearance, FullCheck.
"""

